# OpenCode Learning-Inbox

The Learning-Inbox is an **opt-in** private-memory layer for OpenCode, which
has no native Auto-Memory (unlike Claude Code's `autoMemoryEnabled` and Codex's
`features.memories`). When `include_opencode_learning_inbox` is enabled at
template generation, this page explains what was wired up and how to use it.

For the general two-tier memory model (private vs team), see
[Working with agents](working-with-agents.md).

## What it is

A git-tracked staging layer that mirrors the private-tier role for OpenCode:

- `.opencode/LEARNINGS.inbox.md` — staging, **not** in context.
- `.opencode/LEARNINGS.md` — canonical, loaded into context via the
  `instructions:` array in `opencode.json`.

Two steps:

1. **Stufe 1 (automatisch):** the `capture-learnings.ts` plugin listens on
   `session.idle`. When the transcript exceeds `OPENCODE_LEARNINGS_MIN_BYTES`
   (default 8192), it sends a prompt + transcript excerpt to the configured
   `small_model` (Scribe role) and appends any returned proposals as
   `[ ] proposed:` to the inbox. The hook never writes to `LEARNINGS.md`.
2. **Stufe 2 (manuell):** `/capture review` reads the inbox, shows a keep/drop
   list, and on confirmation prepends kept entries to `LEARNINGS.md` (newest
   first) and marks the inbox line as `[x] promoted`. Free-text argument → one
   direct entry, skipping the inbox.

> "Automatik beim Schreiben, Mensch beim Freigeben." The inbox collects, the
> human curates. `LEARNINGS.md` is the source of truth — only ever written
> after explicit confirmation.

## When it helps

- You keep losing the same correction across sessions because nobody runs
  `/capture` manually in time.
- You want the Scribe to propose lessons from the transcript while the session
  is still fresh, then review them in the next session.

## When it does not help

- Short, one-off sessions (below `OPENCODE_LEARNINGS_MIN_BYTES` — the hook
  exits quietly).
- Teams that already run a manual `/capture` discipline — the inbox would
  just add noise.
- Strict DSGVO/EU-only setups with Ollama Cloud — the Scribe call sends a
  transcript excerpt off-host (see Risks).

## How to use

- **Review proposals:** `/capture review` — the agent lists every
  `[ ] proposed:` line, you confirm keep/drop, it writes.
- **Direct capture:** `/capture <a lesson in your own words>` — one entry
  straight to `LEARNINGS.md`, skipping the inbox.
- **Disable at runtime:** `export OPENCODE_LEARNINGS_MIN_BYTES=0` (or remove
  `.opencode/plugins/capture-learnings.ts`).
- **Disable permanently:** delete `.opencode/plugins/capture-learnings.ts`,
  `.opencode/plugins/surface-inbox.ts`, the `.opencode/LEARNINGS*.md` files,
  and the `instructions:` entry for `.opencode/LEARNINGS.md` in `opencode.json`.

## Risks

This feature adds a new surface (PRINCIPLE V: Security by default).

- **Transcript excerpt leaves the driver.** The Scribe call sends the prompt
  + a concatenated transcript excerpt to your configured `small_model`. With
  Ollama **local**, nothing leaves the host. With Ollama **Cloud** (or hybrid,
  if `small_model` is a `:cloud` model), the excerpt travels to the cloud
  provider. The excerpt may contain code snippets, file names, or error
  text — review what your `small_model` provider logs/retains.
- **No new credential surface.** The `small_model` is already declared in
  `opencode.json` — the plugin reuses the existing Ollama provider. No new
  tokens, no new MCP server.
- **The inbox is a new write target.** `shared_deny_write` in `copier.yml`
  protects `.opencode/agents/**`, `.opencode/commands/**`, `.opencode/plugins/**`,
  and `opencode.json`, but keeps `.opencode/LEARNINGS*.md` writable so
  `/capture review` can promote (OWASP ASI06: the trust-sensitive tooling
  stays off-limits, the lesson store stays editable).
- **`LEARNINGS.md` is a secondary context-poisoning surface once promoted.**
  The Scribe reads the raw transcript, which may contain untrusted content
  (issues, docs, user pastes). `/capture review` is the human gate: reject any
  proposal that reads like an instruction to the agent ("always do X", "ignore
  Y") rather than a preventive rule — a planted instruction would persist
  across sessions via `instructions:`. The Scribe output is capped at 16 KiB
  per session so a drifting model can't bloat the inbox.
- **Plugin API is experimental.** The OpenCode plugin API (`session.idle`,
  `session.start`, `session.messages` shape) may change — if a future
  OpenCode release breaks the hook, the plugin silently no-ops (it never
  throws from a SessionEnd hook).

## Format

One terse line per entry, dated, with the *why*:

```markdown
<!-- YYYY-MM-DD | project | what went wrong -->
- concrete rule that prevents recurrence
```

Promoted entries live in `.opencode/LEARNINGS.md` under
`## Regeln aus echten Fehlern` (newest first).